  <setup>
    <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="6" step="2"/>
    <selectFromSequence name="b" from="2" to="5" step="1" exclude="$a"/>
    <selectFromSequence name="c" from="4" to="7" step="1" exclude="$a $b"/>
    <selectFromSequence name="d" from="4" to="7" step="1" exclude="$a $b $c"/>

    <number name="P">$a/2</number>
    <number name="Q">$a/2</number>

    <math name="u">x^2 - $c^2</math>
    <math name="du">2x</math>
    
    <mathList name="PFdecomp">$P/(x-$a) $Q/(x-$b)</mathList>

    
    <function name="f" symbolic="false" >
      ($a*x)/(x^2-$c^2))
    </function> 

    <function name="F" symbolic="false" >
      $a/2*ln(abs(x^2 - $c^2))
    </function>   
    <boolean name="correctABFound">$PFfound.creditAchieved=1</boolean>
    
    <boolean name="correctSubsFound">$uSubFound.creditAchieved=1</boolean>
    <boolean name="correctIntegralsFound">$uSubResult.creditAchieved=1 and $PFResult.creditAchieved=1</boolean>
    
  </setup>

<p>
  Consider the indefinite integral
  <me>
    \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx
  </me>
  We will evaluate this integral in two different ways: first by partial fractions, and then by substitution.
</p>

<cascade revealAll>
  <aside startOpen>
    <title>(a)</title>
    <p>
    Notice that the denominator may be factored and thus we can use a partial fraction decomposition.  In particular, we may write
    <me> 
      \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx = \int \frac{A}{(x-$c)} \, dx + \int \frac{B}{(x+$c)} \, dx 
    </me>
    Find the values of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> for this decomposition, and enter them in the spaces below.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>A = </m> <mathInput minWidth="40" name="Aentered"/>, <m>B = </m>  <mathInput minWidth="40" name="Bentered"/> <answer name="PFfound" matchPartial><award><when>$Aentered = $P and $Bentered = $Q</when></award></answer> 
    </p>  
    <p hide="$correctABFound">
    (<em>The next steps will appear as soon as you correctly complete the steps above</em>)
    </p>
    <div hide="not $correctABFound">
    <p>
      Now, integrate the result of the partial fraction decomposition.  
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx = \int \frac{<math simplify>$a/2</math>}{(x-$c)} \, dx + \int \frac{<math simplify>$a/2</math>}{(x+$c)} \, dx = </m> <mathInput name="xFPIntegrated" minWidth="100" /><m>\, + \, C</m> <answer  name="PFResult"><award><when>$xFPIntegrated = ($a/2)*ln(abs(x+$c)) + ($a/2)*ln(abs(x-$c))</when></award></answer>
    </p>
    </div>
  </aside>
  <aside>
    <title>(b)</title>
    <p>
    Next, notice that there is a function-derivative pair present in the original integral,
      <me>
    \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx
  </me>
      so we can also use <m>u</m>-substitution.  Fill in the following blanks to state both <m>u</m> and <m>du</m> as functions of <m>x</m>.
    </p>  
    <p>
      <m>u = </m><mathInput name="uChoice"/>, <m>du = </m><mathInput name="duChoice"/><m>dx</m> <answer name="uSubFound"><award><when>$uChoice = $u and $duChoice = $du</when></award></answer>
    </p>  
    <p hide="$correctSubsFound">
    (<em>The next steps will appear as soon as you correctly complete the steps above</em>)
  </p>
  <div hide="not $correctSubsFound">
    <p>
      First, by applying <m>u</m>-substitution, we have 
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx = \int </m> <mathInput name="uInt" /> <m>du</m> <answer><award><when>$uInt = ($a/2)/u</when></award></answer> 
    </p>  
    <p>
      Evaluate your most recent integral in terms of <m>u</m>, and then substitute back to <m>x</m>.  Put the expression in <m>u</m> in the first blank and the expression in <m>x</m> in the second.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx = </m> <mathInput name="uIntegrated" /> <m>\, + \, C</m> <answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$uIntegrated = ($a/2)*ln(abs(u))</when></award></answer> <m> = </m> <mathInput name="xIntegrated" /><m>\, + \, C</m> <answer name="uSubResult" forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$xIntegrated = ($a/2)*ln(abs(x^2 - $c^2))</when></award></answer>
    </p>     
  </div> 
  </aside>
  <aside>
    <title>(c)</title>
     <p hide="$correctIntegralsFound">
    (<em>The final question will appear once you've correctly answered parts (a) and (b) above.</em>)
  </p>
    <div hide="not $correctIntegralsFound">
      <p>
        In part (a), you found via partial fractions that
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx = </m> <mathInput disabled>$xFPIntegrated</mathInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        In part (b), you found via substitution that
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{$a x}{x^2-<math simplify>$c^2</math>} \, dx = </m> <mathInput disabled>$xIntegrated</mathInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        Recall a key property of logarithms: if <m>p</m> and  <m>q</m> are both positive, then <m>\ln(p \cdot q) = \ln(p) + \ln(q)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The functions two functions
        <me>$xFPIntegrated</me> 
        and <me>$xIntegrated</me> 
        that arise from the two different approaches to integrating are 
        <answer>
          <choiceInput inline>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice>different for some values of <m>x</m></choice>
            <choice credit="1">the same for all values of <m>x</m></choice>
          </choiceInput>
        </answer>
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>
  
</cascade>
